+# Spec: Mid-cycle plan changes
+
+Status: resolved
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Customers cannot change their plan or interval. When someone wants a bigger plan, a smaller plan, or to move between monthly and annual billing, support has to cancel the subscription and start a new one by hand. That is slow and error-prone, it loses the subscription's history, and nothing guarantees the customer isn't charged twice for days they already paid for or charged for days they never got.
+
+## Solution
+
+The billing service gains a single operation for a **plan change**. The customer (via whatever calls the billing package) names the plan they want and, optionally, the interval; the subscription is kept and moved.
+
+- An **upgrade** (a plan with a strictly higher monthly list price than the current plan) takes effect the same day. The customer is invoiced immediately for the **proration**: the new plan's price for the days left in the current billing period, minus a credit for the old plan's unused days. The renewal date does not move.
+- A **downgrade** (a different plan with a lower or equal monthly list price) and an **interval switch** do not change anything today. They become the subscription's **scheduled change**, applied at the next renewal, which bills the new plan and interval at full price. Nothing is billed, credited or refunded at the time of the request.
+- An upgrade combined with an interval switch is split: the upgrade happens now, the interval switch at the next renewal.
+- A subscription in its **trial** changes plan and/or interval immediately, with nothing billed and the trial end unchanged.
+- Asking for the plan and interval the customer already has cancels a scheduled change, if there is one.
+
+The rationale for this timing model is recorded in ADR-0001 (plan change timing).
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As a customer on a monthly plan, I want to upgrade to a higher plan mid-period, so that I get the bigger plan's features today without waiting for my renewal.
+2. As a customer who upgrades, I want to be credited for the unused days of my old plan, so that I never pay twice for the same days.
+3. As a customer who upgrades, I want to be charged for the new plan only for the days left in my current billing period, so that I don't pay for days already behind me.
+4. As a customer who upgrades, I want my renewal date to stay the same, so that my billing remains predictable.
+5. As a customer who upgrades, I want the invoice to show the credit for my old plan and the charge for my new plan as separate lines, so that I can understand what I'm paying.
+6. As a customer who upgrades on the first day of my billing period, I want the full old price credited and the full new price charged, so that the day of the change is treated like any other day.
+7. As a customer who upgrades on the last day of my billing period, I want a credit and charge for exactly one day, so that the last day is still treated fairly.
+8. As a customer on an annual plan, I want to upgrade mid-year and be prorated at annual prices, so that the credit and charge reflect what I actually pay.
+9. As a customer on an annual plan, I want my upgrade to be recognised as an upgrade based on the plans' monthly list prices, so that the classification is the same whatever interval I'm on.
+10. As a customer on an annual plan upgrading to a plan whose annual price is equal to or lower than my current one, I want the proration invoice issued exactly as calculated (even if it totals zero or less), so that I am not overcharged because of how the price list is set.
+11. As a customer, I want to downgrade to a cheaper plan and keep my current plan until the end of the period I already paid for, so that I get what I paid for.
+12. As a customer who downgrades, I want nothing billed, refunded or credited when I make the request, so that there are no surprise charges or confusing credits.
+13. As a customer who downgrades, I want my next renewal to move me to the cheaper plan and bill its full price, so that I start paying less from my next period.
+14. As a customer switching to a different plan with the same monthly price, I want the switch to happen at my next renewal with nothing billed now, so that an equal-price swap never produces a mid-period charge.
+15. As a monthly customer, I want to switch to annual billing at my next renewal, so that my next invoice covers a full year at the annual price.
+16. As an annual customer, I want to switch to monthly billing at my next renewal, so that I'm not refunded or credited mid-year and simply start monthly billing when my year ends.
+17. As a customer switching interval, I want my new billing period to start on my old renewal date and last one full new interval, so that there is no gap or overlap.
+18. As a customer upgrading and switching interval in the same request, I want the upgrade now (prorated at my current interval's prices) and the interval switch at my next renewal, so that each part follows its own rule.
+19. As a customer with a scheduled change, I want to be able to see which plan and interval I'll move to at my next renewal, so that I know what's coming.
+20. As a customer with a scheduled downgrade, I want a new plan change request to replace it, so that only my latest decision counts.
+21. As a customer with a scheduled downgrade who then upgrades, I want the upgrade to apply now and the scheduled downgrade to be dropped, so that I'm not unexpectedly downgraded at renewal.
+22. As a customer with a scheduled change who upgrades and switches interval, I want the scheduled change replaced by just that interval switch on the new plan, so that the outcome matches my latest request.
+23. As a customer with a scheduled change, I want my upgrade or downgrade judged against the plan I'm currently billed on, not the scheduled one, so that the outcome is predictable.
+24. As a customer with a scheduled change, I want to cancel it by asking for my current plan and interval, so that my renewal continues on my current plan.
+25. As a customer with nothing scheduled, I want a request for the plan and interval I already have to be rejected, so that I know nothing happened.
+26. As a customer in a free trial, I want any plan or interval change to take effect immediately with nothing billed, so that I can try the plan I actually want.
+27. As a customer in a free trial, I want my trial end date unchanged by a plan change, so that changing plan neither extends nor shortens my trial.
+28. As a customer in a free trial who changed plan, I want my first paid period billed on the new plan and interval when the trial ends, so that I pay for what I chose.
+29. As a customer on a retired plan, I want to move to any active plan like any other customer, so that I am not stuck on a plan that's no longer sold.
+30. As a customer on a retired plan, I want a request to switch only my interval to be rejected as not available, so that retired plans aren't sold on new terms.
+31. As a customer, I want a request for a retired plan to be rejected as not available, so that retired plans are not sold to me.
+32. As a customer, I want a request for a plan that doesn't exist to be rejected as not found, so that typos don't silently do something.
+33. As a customer whose subscription is canceled, I want a plan change to be rejected as not active, so that a canceled subscription isn't revived by accident.
+34. As a caller of the billing package, I want a request for an unknown subscription rejected as not found, so that I can report the right error.
+35. As a caller of the billing package, I want a plan change rejected when the subscription's current period has ended but not yet been renewed, so that only the renewal job bills new periods and proration always applies to a paid period.
+36. As a customer with a scheduled downgrade to a plan that is retired before my renewal, I want the renewal to still move me to it, so that the change I was promised is honoured.
+37. As a customer whose subscription missed several renewals, I want a scheduled change applied only at the first catch-up renewal, so that later periods are billed on the plan I moved to.
+38. As a caller of the billing package, I want the change operation to return the invoice it issued, or nothing when nothing was billed, so that I can show the customer what they were charged.
+39. As a finance user, I want every proration invoice recorded in the store like every other invoice and dated on the day of the change, so that the books are complete.
+40. As a finance user, I want each proration line rounded to cents half up on its own, so that amounts follow the package's money convention and can be checked line by line.
+41. As a support agent, I want plan changes handled by the billing package, so that I no longer cancel and resubscribe customers by hand.
+42. As a caller of the billing package, I want a dedicated invalid-plan-change error for requests that can't be honoured, alongside the existing errors where they fit, so that I can handle each failure correctly.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+- **Public contract.** `BillingService.change_plan(subscription_id: str, new_plan_code: str, new_interval: Interval | None = None) -> Invoice | None`. `new_interval=None` means the subscription's current interval. It returns the invoice issued for the change, or `None` when nothing is billed at the moment of the request.
+- **New error.** `InvalidPlanChange`, a subclass of `BillingError`, defined with the other domain errors and exported from the package alongside them.
+- **Validation order.** Each check runs only if the earlier ones pass:
+  1. Unknown subscription → `SubscriptionNotFound`.
+  2. Canceled subscription → `SubscriptionNotActive`.
+  3. Unknown target plan → `PlanNotFound`.
+  4. The current period has ended without being renewed (period end on or before today, trials included) → `InvalidPlanChange`.
+  5. Target is the current plan and current interval: if a scheduled change exists, clear it and return `None`; otherwise → `InvalidPlanChange`.
+  6. Target plan is retired → `PlanNotAvailable`. This includes switching interval while staying on a retired current plan.
+- **Trialing subscriptions.** The plan and interval become the target immediately. The trial start and end dates do not change. No invoice is issued and the call returns `None`. The existing trial-end renewal then bills the first paid period on the new plan and interval.
+- **Classification (active subscriptions).** Compare the target plan's monthly list price with the current plan's (the plan being billed now, never the scheduled one), regardless of either interval.
+  - Strictly higher → **upgrade**.
+  - Otherwise, a different plan → **downgrade**.
+  - The same plan with a different interval → **interval switch** only.
+- **Upgrade.**
+  - The subscription's plan becomes the target plan today. Its interval and billing period dates are unchanged.
+  - An invoice dated today has two lines, both priced at the current interval:
+    - a negative credit line for the old plan's unused days;
+    - a positive charge line for the new plan's remaining days.
+  - Both lines cover the same days, from today (inclusive) to the period end (exclusive). Each amount is `list price × remaining days ÷ days in the billing period`, computed exactly and then rounded half up to cents on its own.
+  - Line descriptions name the plan, the interval and the date range, consistent with the existing period lines.
+  - The invoice is issued and returned whatever its total, including zero or negative.
+  - Scheduled change afterwards: if the request also asked for a different interval, it becomes (target plan, requested interval); otherwise it is cleared.
+- **Downgrade and/or interval switch.** The scheduled change becomes (target plan, target interval), replacing any earlier one. The plan, interval and billing period are untouched. No invoice is issued and the call returns `None`.
+- **Scheduled change on the subscription.**
+  - The subscription gets two new optional fields, the scheduled plan code and the scheduled interval. They are either both empty or both set, and default to empty so existing constructions keep working.
+  - At most one scheduled change exists at a time. Every request leaves either nothing scheduled or only what that request implies.
+- **Renewal.**
+  - When a subscription with a scheduled change renews, the new billing period starts on the old period end and lasts one interval of the scheduled interval.
+  - The plan and interval become the scheduled ones and the scheduled change is cleared. The renewal invoice bills the scheduled plan's full price for the scheduled interval.
+  - This still applies if the scheduled plan has been retired since the request.
+  - When catching up several missed renewals, only the first one applies the change; later ones renew normally on the new plan.
+- **Canceling.** Canceling does not need to touch a scheduled change, because canceled subscriptions are never renewed.
+- **Docs.** The package README's description of the billing service lists the new operation. `GLOSSARY.md` and ADR-0001 are already up to date.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the public `BillingService` interface.** Tests use the real in-memory store and a fixed clock, exactly like the existing service tests. The proration math and the classification stay private and are tested only through `change_plan` and `renew_due`.
+- **Good tests check external behaviour only:**
+  - the return value (an invoice or `None`);
+  - the error type raised;
+  - the subscription read back from the store: plan, interval, status, period dates and scheduled change;
+  - the invoices recorded for it: issue date, line amounts and total.
+- **Prior art.** The existing service tests: subscribe-then-assert-invoice, moving the clock and calling `renew_due`, trial-end renewal, and errors checked with `pytest.raises`. Their fixtures are plans `basic` (10/100) and `pro` (30/300) with the clock starting on 2026-01-15. Tests may add plans locally, for example:
+  - a plan with an inverted annual price, such as monthly 35 / annual 280;
+  - an equal-monthly-price plan.
+- **Scenarios to cover:**
+  - **Upgrade, monthly.** Basic monthly from Jan 15 to Feb 15 (31 days), upgraded to Pro on Jan 25 (21 days left). Expect a credit of −6.77 (10 × 21/31 = 6.774…), a charge of 20.32 (30 × 21/31 = 20.322…) and a total of 13.55. The invoice is dated Jan 25 and recorded in the store. The period is unchanged and the plan is Pro.
+  - **Upgrade on the first and last day of the period.** On the first day, the full old price is credited and the full new price charged. On the last day, both lines cover one day.
+  - **Upgrade, annual,** including a period that contains Feb 29, so the period has 366 days.
+  - **Upgrade with an inverted annual price.** The invoice is issued with a zero or negative total and the plan changes today.
+  - **Upgrade combined with an interval switch.** Proration uses the current interval, the scheduled change is (new plan, new interval), and the renewal applies the new interval at full price.
+  - **Downgrade.** The call returns `None` with no invoice and the plan unchanged until renewal. The renewal moves the subscription to the cheaper plan and bills its full price.
+  - **Equal monthly price, different plan.** This behaves like a downgrade.
+  - **Interval switch, both directions.** The call returns `None`. The renewal starts a period of the new interval on the old period end and bills the full price.
+  - **A later request replaces a scheduled change:**
+    - a downgrade replaced by an interval switch;
+    - a downgrade dropped by a plain upgrade;
+    - a downgrade replaced by an upgrade combined with an interval switch.
+  - **Classification ignores the scheduled change.** It compares against the current plan.
+  - **Asking for the current plan and interval.** With something scheduled, this cancels it and returns `None`. With nothing scheduled, it raises `InvalidPlanChange`.
+  - **Trial.** A plan or interval change applies immediately with no invoice and `None` returned, and the trial end is unchanged. At trial end, the first paid period is billed on the new plan and interval.
+  - **Errors.** Unknown subscription; canceled subscription; unknown plan; retired target plan; interval switch on a retired current plan; an ended, unrenewed period, for both an active subscription and a trial.
+  - **Moving off a retired plan.** A customer on a retired plan can upgrade or downgrade to an active plan.
+  - **Scheduled plan retired before the renewal.** The renewal still applies it.
+  - **Catch-up renewals.** The scheduled change applies only to the first missed renewal.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Prorating downgrades or interval switches, and mid-period refunds or credits of any kind.
+- A customer credit balance, or carrying credits to later invoices.
+- Collecting payment or issuing refunds. This package only issues invoices.
+- Plan change history or an audit log beyond the invoices and the subscription's current state.
+- Choosing an effective date other than today (now) or the next renewal.
+- More than one scheduled change at a time.
+- Quantity- or seat-based pricing, discounts, coupons and taxes.
+- Reactivating canceled subscriptions.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Domain vocabulary follows `GLOSSARY.md`: plan change, upgrade, downgrade, interval switch, scheduled change, proration, billing period, trial, retired plan.
+- ADR-0001 records why only upgrades apply immediately and why the monthly list price alone decides upgrade vs downgrade. It also records the known effect that an annual upgrade can prorate to a zero or negative total.
